  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "adr-multi-tenancy-strategy-1",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "ADR: Pooled tenancy by default, siloed for enterprise",
      "width": 180,
      "height": 52,
      "collapsed": false,
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "status": "proposed",
      "date": "",
      "context": "The product serves many customers (tenants) of very different sizes. Every tenant needs its data isolated from every other tenant; not every tenant needs — or can afford to pay for — the operational cost of a fully dedicated stack.",
      "decision": "Small and mid-size tenants share infrastructure in a pooled model: one database, every table carrying a tenant_id, with row-level security enforcing isolation at the query layer. Enterprise tenants that require it (contractually or for compliance) are provisioned a siloed, dedicated database instead, behind the same application tier.",
      "consequences": "• Pooled tenants get lower cost and simpler operations (one schema to migrate, one fleet to patch), at the cost of relying on row-level security being correct everywhere — a single missing tenant_id filter is a cross-tenant data leak.\n• Siloed tenants get the strongest isolation story and simplest compliance narrative, at the cost of per-tenant operational overhead (migrations, backups, capacity planning multiply by tenant count).\n• The application tier must support both storage topologies behind one code path, which the tenant-resolution layer needs to route transparently.\n• Moving a tenant from pooled to siloed later requires a live data migration, so the trigger for that move (contract tier, compliance requirement) should be decided up front.",
      "alternatives": "1. Always siloed — safest isolation story, but operational cost scales linearly with tenant count and makes a low-cost self-serve tier uneconomical.\n2. Always pooled — cheapest to run, weakest isolation story; unattractive to enterprise buyers with compliance requirements.\n3. Schema-per-tenant — a middle ground, but migration tooling has to run per schema, and most databases don't scale well past a few thousand schemas.\n4. Pooled by default, siloed for enterprise (chosen) — matches cost to what each tier actually needs."
    }
  ]
}
